// SPIM3 at 0x4002F000, absent from Renode's nRF52840 platform, with the SX1262
// on it.
//
// This is the controller the RAK4631's radio is actually wired to, and the one
// the Adafruit core's SPI object drives on this part. Renode declares spi0..2
// and stops, so every transfer the firmware made went to an address with no
// peripheral behind it: reads returned zero, EVENTS_END never arrived, and it
// polled 0x118 for ever.
//
// It looked like a crypto engine because 0x4002F000 is easy to misread as
// CryptoCell. The register offsets are what settled it - 0x118 EVENTS_END,
// 0x304 INTENSET, 0x308 INTENCLR are a SPIM, not a crypto block.
spi3: SPI.NRF52840_SPI @ sysbus 0x4002F000
    easyDMA: true
    -> nvic@0x2F

lora: Radio.RadioServerSX1262 @ spi3
    host: "127.0.0.1"
    port: 7777
    // DIO1 into the MCU. The RAK4631 wires it to P1.15, which is pin 47 in the
    // flat numbering the Adafruit core uses and pin 15 of gpio1 here. Without
    // it the firmware configures the radio and then waits on a pin nothing
    // drives, which looks exactly like a radio that never receives.
    IRQ -> gpio1@15

// Chip select, from the pin RadioLib actually drives.
//
// Renode's SPI model calls Transmit() for every byte and never calls
// FinishTransmission(), so the chip never sees a transaction boundary - it
// took 3,320 bytes and executed not one command, because VirtualSX1262 acts on
// a command when chip select is released. RadioLib drives NSS as an ordinary
// GPIO rather than letting the controller do it, so the boundary is here: P1.10
// on a RAK4631, which is pin 42 flat and pin 10 of gpio1.
gpio1:
    10 -> lora@0
